the foundation move for phase 3: MCP_Tool (and MCP_Resource) leave
the global Synchronized.var and become context entities — a
Name_Space table (registration) plus a name set (activation) in
Generic_Data. everything later (params, bundles, antiquotations,
tool_scope) builds on this. no new mcp tool in this plan; the
client-visible behavior of tools/list and tools/call must be
UNCHANGED for the default configuration (regression gate: test_mcp.py
and all existing suites stay green).

prototype: the spike theory (2026-07-11) validated the exact shape —
Name_Space.define/check/extern in Generic_Data, an add/del
declaration attribute, [[...]] in bundles, `declare [[x del: n]]` at
theory level. this plan is that prototype plus production concerns
(Local_Theory declaration path, compat, protocol commands).

data model (MCP_Tools.thy)
--------------------------

  structure Data = Generic_Data
  (
    type T = tool Name_Space.table * Symtab.set   (* defs, active *)
    val empty = (Name_Space.empty_table "mcp_tool", Symtab.empty)
    fun merge ((t1, a1), (t2, a2)) =
      (Name_Space.merge_tables (t1, t2), Symtab.merge (K true) (a1, a2))
  )

  type tool =
    {description: string,
     params: param list,      (* [] until plans/mcp_tool_command *)
     form: form,              (* string_fun | diag_wrap | method_wrap *)
     run: Proof.context -> (string * string) list -> string}

merge semantics DECISION (pinned by test, not just precedent): both
components merge by union; a del on one import path + add on another
resurrects the tool. this is standard context-data behavior (simpsets
do the same) and the escape hatch is a del in the importing theory.

api (mirrors named_theorems.ML)
--------------------------------

  MCP_Tool.declare: binding -> string -> ... -> local_theory ->
    string * local_theory        (* the one entry point; emits
                                    MCP.tools_changed at the end *)
  MCP_Tool.check: Proof.context -> xstring * Position.T -> string
                                 (* Name_Space.check + completion *)
  MCP_Tool.get / list / active: context-relative reads
  MCP_Tool.activate / deactivate: string -> generic -> generic
  MCP_Tool.run: Proof.context -> string -> (string*string) list -> string

  attribute (Attrib.setup, spike-validated syntax):
    declare [[mcp_tool add: name]] / [[mcp_tool del: name]]

  diag command: print_mcp_tools ("!" = include inactive), mirroring
  print_bundles: name, active?, form, one-line description, position.

  ONE API (decided 2026-07-11): MCP_Tool.declare is the only
  registration entry point; MCP_Tool.register is DELETED, not
  wrapped. no compat layer — a second signature for the same
  operation is exactly the kind of parallel state this pivot exists
  to remove. every existing registration site is ported in this plan
  (step 2 below); anything specced against register (nothing is —
  the mcp_tool command in plans/mcp_tool_command was already specced
  onto the registry's declaration path) targets declare from the
  start. registration replay on re-execution is naturally idempotent
  (Name_Space.define in a re-run theory re-defines).

protocol commands (same file)
------------------------------

MCP.tools / MCP.run_tool / MCP.resources / MCP.read_resource gain a
designation argument (first chunk): a canonical theory long name, or
"" = the server's -T default. the handler resolves it via
Thy_Info.get_theory -> Proof_Context.init_global and reads
registered-AND-active tools of that context. (repl designation comes
with plans/tool_scope — the ML side here only needs theory
designation; keep the dispatcher ready to grow a "repl:ID" form.)
payload rows grow to (exposed base data): name = full internal name,
description, form tag; scala externs/sanitizes (see plans/tool_scope).

